{% sec 1, "The Bank Behind the Banks", "What is a central bank, in plain words?", "The bank behind the banks" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think of the whole economy as a house, and the {% gl "central bank" %}central bank{% endgl %} as the person in charge of the thermostat. When the house is too cold, businesses close and people lose their jobs. When it's too hot, everyone spends at once, shops can't keep up, and prices start rising fast: that's {% gl "inflation" %}inflation{% endgl %}. The central bank can't change the weather outside (a war, a pandemic, a bad harvest), but it can turn the heating up or down.</p>
    <p>Its dial is the interest rate. Lower rates make borrowing cheaper and saving less rewarding, so people and companies spend and invest more: the heating goes up. Higher rates make loans more expensive and saving more attractive, so spending cools. Using that dial is called {% gl "monetary policy" %}monetary policy{% endgl %}.</p>
    <h3>Its five jobs</h3>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>It sets the price of money.<span class="why">Each central bank sets one short-term {% gl "policy rate" %}policy rate{% endgl %}, the rate banks earn or pay when they deal with it overnight. Every other rate in the country (mortgages, savings accounts, business loans, government bonds) is built on top of it.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>It's the bank for banks.<span class="why">You keep your money at a bank; banks keep theirs at the central bank. Those accounts, called reserves, are how banks pay each other when you send money from your bank to a friend's.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>It issues the currency.<span class="why">The notes in your wallet are a promise from the central bank. In most countries it's the only institution allowed to create them.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>It's the {% gl "lender of last resort" %}lender of last resort{% endgl %}.<span class="why">When banks panic and stop lending to each other, the central bank lends to sound banks against good collateral so a scare doesn't become a collapse. Crashes That Changed the Rules shows it at work in 2008 and 2020.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">5</span><span>It often supervises the banks.<span class="why">In the UK, the Bank of England's Prudential Regulation Authority checks that banks hold enough capital; the ECB does this for the biggest euro area banks; the CBN licenses and supervises Nigerian banks and many fintechs. It's also usually the government's own bank.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>The loop it sits in</h3>
    <p>The central bank watches four numbers: how much the economy produces (GDP), how fast prices rise (inflation), how many people have jobs, and interest rates. They are tied together in a loop, and the central bank's dial is the only part of it that someone controls directly:</p>
  </div>
  <ol class="cb-loop" aria-label="How growth, jobs, prices and interest rates feed into each other">
    <li>The economy grows: companies sell more and hire more people.</li>
    <li>With fewer people looking for work, employers have to pay more to hire and keep staff.</li>
    <li>Higher pay means more spending, and companies pass higher wage costs on in their prices.</li>
    <li>Inflation rises above the central bank's target.</li>
    <li class="cb-bank">The central bank raises interest rates.</li>
    <li>Borrowing costs more, so people and companies spend and invest less.</li>
    <li class="cb-back">Growth slows, hiring slows, inflation cools, and the bank can cut rates again.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Each step takes time. A rate rise today mostly affects spending and prices a year or more from now, a delay the How a Rate Change Travels note covers properly. That's why central banks act on forecasts, not just on today's numbers.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>A central bank can't make anyone spend, hire or lower their prices. It changes the price of money, and the promise of what it will do next, then waits for millions of people to change their behaviour.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 2, "What They're Told to Aim For", "What is a mandate, and why is the target 2%?", "Mandates" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A central bank doesn't choose its own goals. Its government or parliament gives it a job description, called a <strong>mandate</strong>, and then (in most rich countries) leaves it alone to decide how to meet it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A {% gl "dual mandate" %}dual mandate{% endgl %}:</strong> the US Federal Reserve was told by Congress in 1977 to aim for maximum employment and stable prices, with equal weight.</li>
      <li><strong>Prices first:</strong> the European Central Bank's treaty makes price stability its main aim; supporting the EU's wider economic goals only comes after that. The Bank of England is the same: a <strong>2%</strong> target set by the government, with supporting growth and jobs as a secondary objective.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The difference matters most when the goals clash. If unemployment is high and inflation low, every central bank cuts. But if inflation is high <em>and</em> people are losing jobs (this is called {% gl "stagflation" %}stagflation{% endgl %}), a bank told to put prices first will keep fighting inflation, while one with a dual mandate has to weigh the job losses too.</p>
    <h3>Why 2%, and not zero?</h3>
    <p>Almost every major central bank aims for an {% gl "inflation target" %}inflation target{% endgl %} of <strong>2%</strong> a year. The Fed made its 2% target official in 2012, the Bank of Japan in 2013, and the ECB settled on "2%, symmetric" in 2021. Why not aim for prices that never change?</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>The official numbers slightly overstate inflation.<span class="why">When a product gets better (a faster phone at the same price), the price index can't fully capture it. So measured inflation of 0% might really be a small fall in prices.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>Falling prices are dangerous.<span class="why">In {% gl "deflation" %}deflation{% endgl %}, people put off buying because things will be cheaper next month, and debts get heavier in real terms. Japan spent much of 1995 to 2012 stuck in it. A cushion of 2% keeps the economy away from that edge.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>It leaves room to cut.<span class="why">Interest rates usually sit a little above inflation. With 2% inflation, rates are normally a few per cent, so the central bank has room to cut in a recession before it hits zero.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>It makes wage adjustments easier.<span class="why">People hate pay cuts. With 2% inflation, a firm in trouble can hold pay flat for a year, which is a small real cut, instead of cutting it.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>2% is low enough that people stop thinking about inflation in their daily lives, which is exactly the point. At 2% a year, prices double in about 35 years; at 10%, in about 7.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
